# Continuous controls and alternatives

Use a continuous control when someone needs to adjust a meaningful range. Keep the native control when it already gives the right keyboard, touch and accessible-value behaviour. A decorative dial is not a substitute for a usable input.

[Sliders and precise values](../components/sliders.html) owns the reusable range, exact-entry and step-button appearance, with five complete examples. This page owns the native capability, commit and reconciliation rules. The slider is a Civica extension. The [UK design systems and Home Assistant controls](../community/uk-design-system-review.html) page records the guidance behind it.

These examples are simulated with invented data. They do not control a home. This page covers the design contract (HA-CTRL-003, from gap HA-GAP-003). It does not add a native adapter.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Domain input | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| Minimum, maximum and step | Use the integration’s range and increments, including decimal steps. Do not reuse a brightness percentage range for temperature or position. |
| Current value and draft | Label the draft separately until the native contract confirms the change. |
| Unit and format | Show a visible unit and an accessible value with meaning, such as “19.5 degrees Celsius”, not a bare number. |
| Commit behaviour | Follow the native component: it either sends changes while dragging or on commit. Do not add a second subscription that duplicates requests. |
| Essential hold or double-tap action | Provide an ordinary visible control, or a route to the native details, for the same task. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Keep valid minimum and maximum values, and never use a minimum to mean unknown. When a value is missing, replace the numeric control with an explanation and keep the device name. If the integration changes its supported range during an edit, keep the draft for review and explain the new limit. Do not silently send a clamped value.

On a control that updates often, keep the local draft apart from incoming reported values. Do not reset the thumb on every unrelated update, which fights the pointer. If a commit fails, show the latest reported value and say the draft was not sent or not confirmed. If a physical switch changes the value during an edit, reconcile the two. Do not claim the local draft won.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Write “Brightness”, “Target temperature” or “Volume” with the device name. Show on and off in words next to a switch state. Offer number entry or step buttons when precision matters and the integration supports it. Do not show several equivalent inputs that can disagree. The percentage example changes only its local draft. It is not a live dimmer.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Check arrow, Home and End behaviour against the native control you chose, and do not promise shortcuts it lacks. Keep a visible focus indicator, large touch targets and a label outside the moving thumb. Keep the slider’s semantic bounds and accessible value. At 320px, 200% text and forced colours, the label and current value must stay clear. Keep focus and input boundaries stronger than quiet card dividers.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

The native slider and card-action contracts are the starting point. The source review recorded `ha-control-slider` at frontend commit `b1164739c3a853a28ba1c425415e96a17eb6286e`. That does not establish compatibility with every installed frontend. A new private adapter needs its own versioned tests. The HTML examples show labels and constraints. They do not replace Home Assistant components.

